      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Recent studies, including the MTA's own evaluation, suggest that while New York City's initial congestion pricing implementation showed a 15% drop in city center taxi traffic and a massive 360,000-vehicle one-time shift to subways, there is ongoing debate about long-term efficiency. Critics argue the financial distribution is biased against public transit-heavy neighborhoods compared to wealthy East Side areas, while proponents highlight the revenues could be better spent on rail and bus improvements. The controversy extends to logistical discussions about enforcement efforts, which would likely rely on new license plate recognition cameras.",
